WEBVTT

00:01.356 --> 00:04.525
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.601 --> 00:07.869
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.903 --> 00:11.431
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.470 --> 00:14.443
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.487 --> 00:17.952
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:17.986 --> 00:21.288
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.330 --> 00:22.851
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:22.892 --> 00:24.185
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.219 --> 00:24.769
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:33.772 --> 00:35.744
You are watching Amazon Prime,

00:35.786 --> 00:38.614
the network that brought you
your sunscreen in two days.

00:38.648 --> 00:42.345
Amazon Prime-- like
the eighth best way to watch TV.

00:42.379 --> 00:44.676
Hi, Chris, how
was school?

00:44.710 --> 00:46.976
Wait, what's that note
pinned to your backpack?

00:47.010 --> 00:48.644
Bet it's from a girl
in the lunchroom

00:48.678 --> 00:50.641
who wants a taste
of corn dog.

00:50.675 --> 00:52.304
Was that a sex joke?

00:52.338 --> 00:53.338
It's...

00:53.344 --> 00:54.707
I don't know,
shut up.

00:54.742 --> 00:57.400
Oh, dear, it's from
Principal Shepherd.

00:57.442 --> 00:59.936
Peter, he wants to see us
first thing tomorrow.

00:59.978 --> 01:01.546
Oh, Chris is
in trouble.

01:01.580 --> 01:03.881
Sounds like he needs a
little fatherly wisdom.

01:03.915 --> 01:05.510
Better get my pipe.

01:05.544 --> 01:07.506
Peter, I think that's
the wrong kind of pipe.

01:07.540 --> 01:08.841
I'm sorry, Mom.

01:08.875 --> 01:10.839
I haven't been doing
too great in school lately.

01:10.881 --> 01:12.508
Well, that's
very disappointing.

01:12.542 --> 01:15.177
I guess your father and I will
have to talk to the principal

01:15.211 --> 01:16.669
and figure out
how to handle this.

01:16.703 --> 01:17.836
Ah, I wouldn't
worry, Lois.

01:17.870 --> 01:19.507
I never did very well
in school either.

01:19.541 --> 01:21.138
I even failed
homeroom.

01:21.172 --> 01:22.241
Peter Griffin?

01:22.275 --> 01:23.375
There.

01:27.005 --> 01:28.970
Thank you for coming,
Mr. and Mrs. Griffin.

01:29.004 --> 01:31.336
Chris seems to be
struggling in his classes,

01:31.378 --> 01:33.670
and often when kids
have trouble in school,

01:33.712 --> 01:35.844
it's because something
is going on at home.

01:35.879 --> 01:38.506
I haven't been attracted to Lois
for a year and a half.

01:38.540 --> 01:41.543
Principal Shepherd, exactly
how poorly is Chris doing?

01:41.577 --> 01:44.540
Well, right now,
he has a D-minus average.

01:44.574 --> 01:46.706
His only hope is
his history final tomorrow.

01:46.740 --> 01:48.041
If he fails that,

01:48.075 --> 01:50.175
I'm afraid he'll have
to repeat ninth grade.

01:50.209 --> 01:53.011
This... seems more
like a phone call.

01:53.045 --> 01:54.170
Oh, my God.

01:54.212 --> 01:56.177
I-I always knew Chris wasn't
a great student,

01:56.211 --> 01:59.005
but I had no idea
the situation was so dire.

01:59.039 --> 02:00.959
Don't worry, I'll make
sure Chris passes history.

02:00.972 --> 02:02.441
You can count on me.

02:02.475 --> 02:05.975
<i>The same way NASA counted on me
during the Apollo 13 crisis.</i>

02:06.009 --> 02:07.476
All right, I'm gonna
need three coffins--

02:07.510 --> 02:08.811
mahogany with
brass handles--

02:08.845 --> 02:10.640
three new daddies for the
dead astronauts' kids,

02:10.674 --> 02:11.975
and a black
suit for me.

02:12.009 --> 02:14.971
Sir, I think I know a way
we can get them back.

02:15.005 --> 02:15.970
Is it hard?

02:16.004 --> 02:17.137
It might be.

02:17.171 --> 02:18.974
Yeah, we-we've kind
of already decided

02:19.008 --> 02:20.341
we're going in
this direction.

02:24.371 --> 02:26.008
All right, Chris,
you got a lot riding

02:26.042 --> 02:27.343
on this history
exam tomorrow.

02:27.377 --> 02:28.343
I know.

02:28.376 --> 02:30.174
Sam Cooke didn't
know much about history,

02:30.208 --> 02:31.677
and he got shot in a hotel

02:31.711 --> 02:34.011
wearing nothing
but a sports coat and a shoe.

02:34.045 --> 02:36.178
Yeah, that's hilarious, Chris,
but we've got to buckle down.

02:36.212 --> 02:38.681
Now, I'm gonna help you study
by nailing your door shut.

02:38.715 --> 02:41.218
That way, you'll have no
choice but to hit the books.

02:42.916 --> 02:45.216
Oh, crap, I left
my phone in there.

02:45.250 --> 02:46.718
Damn
it, it's nailed shut.

02:46.753 --> 02:49.021
Dad, it looks like
some girl sent you

02:49.055 --> 02:50.347
a picture of her privates.

02:50.389 --> 02:52.282
Oh, crap! Lois!
I don't have the phone!

02:52.324 --> 02:53.784
Stop sending pictures!

02:53.818 --> 02:55.550
I already sent four more!

02:57.087 --> 02:58.884
Chris, put down the phone!
Don't look at those!

02:58.918 --> 03:01.323
Wait a minute,
that's both her hands!

03:01.357 --> 03:03.492
Who the hell is
taking the pictures?!

03:03.526 --> 03:05.830
All right, give me that
phone and get back to work.

03:05.864 --> 03:07.199
And before you
judge your mother--

03:07.233 --> 03:09.233
you did most
of that damage.

03:13.108 --> 03:14.577
Oh.

03:14.611 --> 03:15.905
Hey, Chris.

03:15.947 --> 03:17.577
Just wanted to see
how the studying's going.

03:17.611 --> 03:19.577
You guys, I've been
at this all night,

03:19.611 --> 03:21.578
and I haven't
learned a thing.

03:21.619 --> 03:24.417
There's no way I'm gonna pass
Mr. Harpington's history test.

03:24.451 --> 03:25.585
Oh, come on, Chris.

03:25.619 --> 03:27.250
I'm sure that's
just the nerves talking.

03:27.291 --> 03:28.584
Here, I'll quiz you.

03:28.618 --> 03:31.919
Who said, "We have nothing
to fear but fear itself"?

03:31.960 --> 03:33.086
Scooby-Doo?

03:33.128 --> 03:34.254
No.
Shaggy?

03:34.296 --> 03:35.758
<i>It's nobody on Scooby-Doo.</i>

03:35.792 --> 03:37.261
Wow, Chris,
you, uh...

03:37.295 --> 03:38.491
you really don't
know history.

03:39.861 --> 03:42.162
And if I fail ninth grade,
I won't amount to anything.

03:42.196 --> 03:44.160
I'll end up being
one of those bus drivers

03:44.194 --> 03:46.432
that parents don't
completely trust.

03:48.801 --> 03:51.135
You have a beautiful
child, Mrs. Roberts.

03:54.108 --> 03:55.739
God, poor Chris.

03:55.781 --> 03:58.261
He's studying as hard as he can,
but it doesn't seem to matter.

03:58.277 --> 03:59.579
Well, we got to
do something.

03:59.613 --> 04:01.346
If he doesn't pass this
test, he's gonna have to...

04:01.380 --> 04:02.681
Hey.

04:02.715 --> 04:04.184
...he's gonna have to
repeat ninth grade.

04:04.218 --> 04:06.047
Well, there's not much
we can do about it.

04:06.081 --> 04:08.001
Some people just aren't able
to learn from books.

04:08.017 --> 04:10.714
Well, maybe he
doesn't have to.

04:10.748 --> 04:11.748
What do you mean?

04:11.786 --> 04:12.911
Maybe there's a way

04:12.953 --> 04:14.749
to actually show
Chris history...

04:14.783 --> 04:16.084
with my time machine.

04:16.118 --> 04:17.745
You know, that's really
nice of you, Stewie.

04:17.779 --> 04:18.912
Well, he's my brother.

04:18.946 --> 04:20.247
We've got to
stick together.

04:20.281 --> 04:22.717
Like that two-headed
girl on TLC.

04:22.752 --> 04:25.981
Okay, who wants to taste it,
and who wants to smell it?

04:34.542 --> 04:36.148
All right, we'll just
tell Chris he's dreaming,

04:36.182 --> 04:38.411
because I don't want him
to know about my time machine.

04:38.445 --> 04:39.411
Why not?

04:39.445 --> 04:40.850
Eh, it's like having
a pickup truck.

04:40.884 --> 04:42.985
Once people know you got one,
there go your weekends.

04:43.019 --> 04:43.984
Chris?

04:44.018 --> 04:44.984
Can you hear me?

04:45.018 --> 04:45.983
Huh?

04:46.017 --> 04:46.983
You're dreaming.

04:47.017 --> 04:48.656
I'm dreaming?

04:48.690 --> 04:50.689
Yay! No consequences!

04:53.027 --> 04:54.489
So lifelike!

04:54.531 --> 04:57.391
All right, let's go back
six minutes and try this again.

04:57.433 --> 05:00.324
Chris, you're dreaming
with consequences.

05:00.366 --> 05:01.332
What?

05:01.366 --> 05:02.500
That's right.

05:02.534 --> 05:04.500
We're gonna take you on
a special dream trip

05:04.534 --> 05:06.500
to help you pass
your history test.

05:06.534 --> 05:07.500
You are?

05:07.534 --> 05:09.338
Yes, and there's
so much to learn.

05:09.372 --> 05:10.506
For example, did you know

05:10.540 --> 05:13.074
that Jesus didn't really
die on the cross?

05:13.108 --> 05:14.906
So, what was the
cause of death?

05:14.940 --> 05:17.577
Cocaine and severe trauma
to the colon.

05:17.611 --> 05:19.944
Yeah, I'm gonna need
to see those forms.

05:24.481 --> 05:25.783
Don't look at me like that.

05:25.817 --> 05:28.287
Babe, I told you,
it's a business trip.

05:28.321 --> 05:30.224
All right,
I've got the return pad,

05:30.258 --> 05:32.224
and the time machine is
powering up right now.

05:32.258 --> 05:33.559
We should be leaving any moment.

05:33.593 --> 05:35.559
This thing is awesome.

05:35.593 --> 05:37.726
Stewie, can you believe
Brian built this?

05:37.760 --> 05:38.726
Wait, what?

05:38.760 --> 05:41.031
Oh, hey, come on, guys.

05:41.065 --> 05:42.798
"Who built what"
is not a question

05:42.832 --> 05:44.630
on the history
final, right?

05:44.672 --> 05:45.965
Good point, Brian.

05:45.999 --> 05:48.301
That's why victory
is always Brian's.

05:48.335 --> 05:49.804
You dick!

05:49.838 --> 05:52.004
How much of my stuff
have you taken credit for?!

06:05.520 --> 06:06.823
Hey, everybody?

06:06.857 --> 06:09.056
Just so you know,
I'm straight.

06:18.687 --> 06:20.485
All right, Chris, I
reviewed your textbook,

06:20.519 --> 06:22.839
and I've programmed a tour of
the various historical events

06:22.857 --> 06:24.488
that you'll need to
know for your exam.

06:24.522 --> 06:25.826
So, pay close
attention.

06:25.860 --> 06:27.660
First stop--
Washington, D.C.

06:27.701 --> 06:29.164
The year is 1803.

06:29.198 --> 06:31.204
Ah, the future.

06:33.575 --> 06:36.040
Now, the man at the podium
is Thomas Jefferson.

06:36.082 --> 06:37.881
And this is a very
important moment.

06:37.915 --> 06:39.377
He's trying
to convince Congress

06:39.419 --> 06:41.217
to approve
the Louisiana Purchase.

06:41.251 --> 06:42.553
Come on, guys!

06:42.587 --> 06:45.551
We're getting Oklahoma,
Arkansas, Nebraska, Kansas,

06:45.585 --> 06:48.889
Wyoming, North Dakota,
South Dakota and Louisiana.

06:48.923 --> 06:51.290
What are the chances
all those states suck?

06:58.673 --> 07:00.808
This is Paris, 1920.

07:00.842 --> 07:02.978
This bar is where famous
American writers,

07:03.012 --> 07:04.987
like Ernest Hemingway,
would go for a drink.

07:05.021 --> 07:07.084
Oh, my God,
there he is!

07:07.118 --> 07:09.756
Excuse me, Mr. Hemingway,
can I have a beer with you?

07:09.790 --> 07:10.756
Sure!

07:10.790 --> 07:13.123
I love life
and all the people in it!

07:13.157 --> 07:14.157
Thanks.

07:14.164 --> 07:15.362
And, hey, as a
fellow writer,

07:15.396 --> 07:17.426
I would love to talk
to you about the craft.

07:17.460 --> 07:20.435
I mean, God, if only we could
get rid of these damn arms

07:20.469 --> 07:22.636
and attach the pen directly
to the heart, right?

07:25.472 --> 07:27.145
You guys,
I got his pen!

07:30.992 --> 07:32.623
Now, Chris,
this is Brazil

07:32.657 --> 07:34.497
before the invention
of the soccer ball.

07:38.904 --> 07:40.904
Australia before
the shark-tooth necklace.

07:45.482 --> 07:47.112
And Italy
before pasta.

07:47.147 --> 07:48.448
Ay!
Oh!

07:48.483 --> 07:49.784
Vowel sound!

07:49.818 --> 07:51.984
They were always
a ridiculous people.

07:52.018 --> 07:55.655
<i>Hey, Family Guy, you're lucky
I got a manicotti in the oven,</i>

07:55.689 --> 07:58.487
or I'd bash your face
into a bolognese.

07:58.529 --> 08:00.755
That's two kinds of food.

08:00.789 --> 08:02.994
That's how angry I am.

08:12.202 --> 08:13.265
Oh, my God!

08:13.299 --> 08:14.369
Where are we?!

08:14.403 --> 08:16.872
Omaha Beach, 1944.

08:16.906 --> 08:18.368
This is D-Day.

08:18.402 --> 08:19.873
What's that over there?

08:19.907 --> 08:21.136
Oh, that's
Double D-Day.

08:21.178 --> 08:24.640
♪ She's my cherry pie ♪

08:24.682 --> 08:26.817
♪ Cool drink of water,
such a sweet surprise ♪

08:26.851 --> 08:28.145
♪ Tastes so good... ♪

08:28.187 --> 08:31.153
<i>Nein, nein! No
fire! Ist gut!</i>

08:31.187 --> 08:33.153
<i>Heil hooters!</i>

08:33.187 --> 08:34.489
Be careful.

08:34.523 --> 08:36.490
<i>It could be
a booby trap!</i>

08:36.524 --> 08:39.165
Oh, Otto.

08:42.104 --> 08:43.070
Here we are.

08:43.104 --> 08:45.070
Christmas night, 1776.

08:45.104 --> 08:47.069
The father of our nation,
George Washington,

08:47.103 --> 08:49.406
is about to boldly
cross the Delaware.

08:49.440 --> 08:50.574
What's on his arms?

08:50.608 --> 08:51.608
Those are water wings.

08:51.616 --> 08:53.047
He was terrified
of the water.

08:54.753 --> 08:56.151
Easy!

08:59.752 --> 09:02.054
All right, Chris,
it's 1798,

09:02.088 --> 09:03.885
and we're in the home
of Jane Austen.

09:03.927 --> 09:05.388
Jane Austen?

09:05.422 --> 09:07.388
I don't think she's
on my history test, Stewie.

09:07.430 --> 09:08.724
Oh, I know--
we're just here

09:08.758 --> 09:10.564
to release pent-up
time-travel farts.

09:10.598 --> 09:12.061
Awesome!
Yeah!

09:15.598 --> 09:17.404
Aw, yeah!

09:17.438 --> 09:18.731
Give me some of that!

09:18.773 --> 09:20.737
Mmm, the insides
of men!

09:20.770 --> 09:22.734
This is what
fuels me!

09:23.766 --> 09:24.931
Mmm!

09:29.266 --> 09:31.398
Okay, time for some
recent history.

09:31.440 --> 09:33.071
This is the 1990s.

09:33.105 --> 09:37.577
♪ And I'll tell myself
I'm over you... ♪

09:37.611 --> 09:39.577
This is
the entire 1990s?

09:39.611 --> 09:40.814
Yes.

09:46.182 --> 09:47.147
Wait a minute.

09:47.181 --> 09:48.315
Pre-Soviet Russia?

09:48.349 --> 09:49.483
This wasn't
on the manifest.

09:49.517 --> 09:51.251
Yeah, this is kind
of a personal thing.

09:51.285 --> 09:53.756
Hey, Pavlov, you
dog-teasing-dick!

09:53.791 --> 09:55.790
How's this for positive
reinforcement?!

10:04.638 --> 10:06.270
All right,
where to next?

10:06.304 --> 10:08.304
I want to kill
more scientists!

10:13.624 --> 10:14.750
All right, Chris,

10:14.792 --> 10:16.921
that was the British
Parliament, 1912.

10:16.963 --> 10:18.259
Next, we...

10:18.293 --> 10:20.795
I say, that looks like a
young Charlie Chaplin.

10:25.300 --> 10:28.033
Sir, please, take this money and
use it to pursue your dreams.

10:28.074 --> 10:29.400
<i>Danke.</i>

10:30.402 --> 10:32.703
Stewie, I think
that was a young Hitler.

10:32.737 --> 10:34.374
You just gave money
to Hitler!

10:34.408 --> 10:36.374
Yeah, but before all
the crazy stuff.

10:36.408 --> 10:39.375
All right, Chris, let's discuss
what you've learned so far.

10:39.417 --> 10:41.712
Now, what is your biggest
takeaway from our travels?

10:41.754 --> 10:45.151
That you can't take a good nap
with keys in your pocket.

10:45.185 --> 10:46.486
For God's sake, Chris!

10:46.520 --> 10:47.989
You clearly have not
paid attention

10:48.023 --> 10:49.317
to anything we've shown you!

10:49.359 --> 10:51.156
You're absolutely hopeless!

10:51.190 --> 10:53.155
Did you ever consider--
the reason you're having trouble

10:53.189 --> 10:54.658
passing ninth grade is
that you're incapable

10:54.692 --> 10:55.993
of anything else?!

10:56.027 --> 10:58.655
That this is the absolute zenith
of your ability?!

10:58.689 --> 11:00.254
Zenith means top.

11:00.295 --> 11:02.260
No, it means TV.

11:02.294 --> 11:03.996
Ha! Now I'm catching fire.

11:04.030 --> 11:05.996
Damn it, Chris, your problem
isn't book learning,

11:06.030 --> 11:07.500
it's that you're a moron!

11:07.534 --> 11:09.668
This whole trip has been
a complete waste of time!

11:11.534 --> 11:12.836
Screw you, Stewie.

11:12.870 --> 11:14.340
You're a jerk!

11:14.374 --> 11:18.005
And I'm gonna win that bowling
tournament, with or without you!

11:20.710 --> 11:22.347
What is he... what?

11:22.381 --> 11:24.640
He-he doesn't even know what
we're preparing him for!

11:27.678 --> 11:28.812
Chris?

11:28.845 --> 11:30.042
Chris, come back!

11:30.077 --> 11:31.378
Well, we've looked
all over London.

11:31.412 --> 11:32.881
He's nowhere.

11:32.915 --> 11:35.049
I don't know what the
hell we're going to do.

11:35.083 --> 11:36.083
Hey, Stewie!

11:38.079 --> 11:41.413
If I'm such a moron, then how
did I get on a fancy boat?!

11:45.759 --> 11:48.692
See you later, douche bags!

11:48.727 --> 11:49.924
Oh, crap!

11:49.958 --> 11:51.092
<i>Chris is
on the Titanic?!</i>

11:51.126 --> 11:52.428
No, no, it's
okay, Brian.

11:52.462 --> 11:55.029
We'll just go back in time
to before Chris ran off.

11:55.063 --> 11:58.164
Oh, and I took
your fancy smoke detector!

11:59.198 --> 12:00.668
My God, he's got
the return pad!

12:00.702 --> 12:02.437
Brian, we've got to
get on that ship!

12:02.471 --> 12:04.269
Attention, passengers.

12:04.311 --> 12:07.445
The karaoke contest
will begin in five minutes.

12:07.479 --> 12:10.307
Okay, now we've really
got to get on that ship.

12:16.277 --> 12:18.073
Brian, we have
to get on that ship!

12:18.107 --> 12:19.904
It's our only hope
to save Chris.

12:19.938 --> 12:21.408
There! Jump onto
that mooring rope.

12:21.442 --> 12:23.674
We can use it
to pull ourselves up.

12:32.618 --> 12:35.256
Oh, thank God, I wasn't
sure we were gonna make it.

12:35.290 --> 12:36.256
I wasn't worried.

12:36.290 --> 12:37.591
I've faced bigger
challenges.

12:37.625 --> 12:40.294
Like trying to dress
Jonah Hill for the Oscars.

12:41.663 --> 12:42.787
Okay, okay, perfect.

12:42.829 --> 12:44.827
Tell him to stay
exactly that weight.

12:46.828 --> 12:47.793
Yes?

12:47.827 --> 12:49.225
Damn it!

13:16.157 --> 13:18.122
Oh, my God, Brian.

13:18.164 --> 13:20.625
This ship, it's...
it's unbelievable.

13:20.659 --> 13:22.664
It's even more magnificent
than I imagined.

13:23.665 --> 13:24.796
That, Brian.

13:24.830 --> 13:25.963
That.

13:25.997 --> 13:27.427
That's what I was
telling you about.

13:27.461 --> 13:28.699
That's brocade.

13:28.733 --> 13:30.963
Come on, Stewie, we got
to find Chris and the return pad

13:30.997 --> 13:31.963
before it's too late.

13:31.997 --> 13:33.130
All right,
let's split up.

13:33.164 --> 13:34.466
I'll go search
in first class,

13:34.500 --> 13:35.633
you go down
to steerage

13:35.667 --> 13:37.800
and wade through the
gross Irish and Italians.

13:37.834 --> 13:40.269
That's two.

13:40.303 --> 13:43.569
Ugh, God knows what
I'm gonna find in steerage.

13:43.603 --> 13:47.566
Bunch of drunk, fat,
pie-faced Irish fools.

13:47.600 --> 13:50.802
Excuse me, have any of you seen
a blond teenage boy?

13:50.836 --> 13:52.537
Who's asking?

13:52.571 --> 13:54.031
Yeah, who the hell are you?

13:54.073 --> 13:55.798
And I'm the baby!

13:55.840 --> 13:57.837
Chris?

14:04.177 --> 14:05.480
What the hell?

14:05.514 --> 14:07.154
Stewie,
what are you doing?

14:07.188 --> 14:09.153
Uh, dining at the
captain's table.

14:09.187 --> 14:11.318
Everyone at this table
was the first person

14:11.352 --> 14:12.654
to refrigerate
something.

14:12.687 --> 14:14.485
I refrigerated shoes.

14:14.519 --> 14:17.054
Because everybody
likes a cool toe.

14:17.088 --> 14:19.724
Also, I gave the string
quartet the sheet music

14:19.758 --> 14:21.396
for "Highway to
the Danger Zone".

14:21.430 --> 14:23.891
You have the sheet music for
"Highway to the Danger Zone"?

14:23.933 --> 14:25.059
Uh, yeah.

14:25.093 --> 14:27.065
What do you think I
keep in this backpack?

14:27.099 --> 14:28.393
It's all PowerBars
and sheet music.

14:28.435 --> 14:30.064
Stewie, you're supposed
to be looking for Chris.

14:30.098 --> 14:31.894
I did-- he wasn't
in the Turkish bath,

14:31.936 --> 14:34.669
the veranda caf├й or the
Bloody Handkerchief Ball.

14:36.839 --> 14:38.302
Oh, dear.

14:38.336 --> 14:40.972
Relax, Brian, I think
I see Chris right now.

14:41.006 --> 14:42.139
Really? Where?

14:42.173 --> 14:43.969
I'm gonna go ahead
and bet he's the guy

14:44.003 --> 14:45.608
with the whole chicken
stuck on his head.

14:47.641 --> 14:48.943
Hey, you're not Chris.

14:48.977 --> 14:51.441
Pardon me,
that's my hat!

14:53.316 --> 14:55.788
I know this thing's gonna sink,
but I'm having a good time.

14:56.822 --> 14:57.852
Stewie, look!

15:00.461 --> 15:01.754
Chris, there you are!

15:01.788 --> 15:03.425
Well, well, well.

15:03.459 --> 15:06.096
If it isn't Booger Breath
and the Mushroom Tip.

15:06.130 --> 15:08.264
Wh-Which one
of us is which?

15:08.298 --> 15:10.263
Chris, we have got
to get off this boat.

15:10.297 --> 15:12.400
<i>The Titanic was one of the
biggest disasters in history.</i>

15:12.434 --> 15:13.600
Well, if you're
so worried,

15:13.634 --> 15:15.535
why don't you alert
the crew and save everyone?

15:15.569 --> 15:17.703
Because, Chris,
we can't alter the past.

15:17.737 --> 15:19.206
That's the first rule of...

15:19.240 --> 15:21.206
of, uh... uh, dreaming.

15:21.240 --> 15:22.807
Besides, saving
this ship would mean

15:22.841 --> 15:24.112
talking to, like, 80 people,

15:24.146 --> 15:26.449
and I-I'm just not
in that kind of mood right now.

15:26.483 --> 15:28.281
Look, Chris, just give
us that thing you took

15:28.315 --> 15:29.650
so we can
all get home.

15:42.841 --> 15:44.470
Quick, Chris,
Stewie, get on!

15:44.504 --> 15:46.142
To home!

15:46.176 --> 15:48.510
Or I'll just put in
the coordinates.

16:02.296 --> 16:03.262
Damn!

16:03.296 --> 16:04.296
The return pad!

16:04.303 --> 16:05.303
It's been destroyed!

16:05.303 --> 16:06.428
What?!

16:06.470 --> 16:07.763
The whole thing's been
waterlogged!

16:07.797 --> 16:09.930
I'll need to dry it out
and completely rewire it.

16:09.972 --> 16:11.831
This would make
a great movie.

16:11.865 --> 16:13.997
And the parts I need
to fix the return pad

16:14.031 --> 16:15.269
certainly aren't
on this ship.

16:15.303 --> 16:17.100
We have to somehow
get to dry land.

16:17.134 --> 16:18.267
Dry land?!

16:18.301 --> 16:20.265
<i>Stewie, almost everyone
on the Titanic died!</i>

16:20.307 --> 16:21.432
All right,
calm down, Brian.

16:21.474 --> 16:23.438
No-- there's so much
I haven't done.

16:23.472 --> 16:25.068
I've never even
successfully made love

16:25.102 --> 16:26.435
to a woman in
the shower.

16:26.469 --> 16:27.635
Aah, my back!

16:27.669 --> 16:29.804
Should I put my
foot in the soap dish?

16:29.838 --> 16:32.476
Yeah, try that, and maybe
stand on your tippy-toes.

16:32.510 --> 16:35.210
Aah, how does water
make it dry?!

16:42.449 --> 16:43.583
This isn't gonna work.

16:43.617 --> 16:44.920
Brian, this is
our only hope.

16:44.955 --> 16:46.251
The majority
of people

16:46.293 --> 16:48.587
who got on those lifeboats
were women of noble birth.

16:48.629 --> 16:50.426
Now, I've given
us all backstories.

16:50.460 --> 16:52.592
I am Lady Antonia
Tyler Carrington,

16:52.626 --> 16:54.928
the Duchess of Lancashire, the
heir to the Earl of Portsmouth,

16:54.962 --> 16:56.591
and the cousin
of the Countess of Wessex.

16:56.633 --> 16:57.830
Okay, great. And who are we?

16:57.864 --> 16:59.693
I don't know,
you're just two sluts with me.

16:59.735 --> 17:00.861
Hey, there's a lifeboat.

17:00.895 --> 17:02.197
Uh, yeah,
but it's got one

17:02.231 --> 17:04.639
of those funny Southwest
lifeboat attendants.

17:04.673 --> 17:05.968
Hey,
here's a reminder--

17:06.010 --> 17:07.137
as you exit the boat,

17:07.178 --> 17:08.808
don't forget to give us
your extra snacks

17:08.842 --> 17:11.438
because as you know,
we work for peanuts!

17:13.310 --> 17:14.445
That's refreshing.

17:14.479 --> 17:16.382
Most of those guys
are all serious.

17:26.297 --> 17:27.255
There, that one!

17:27.297 --> 17:28.792
It's our last chance.

17:33.439 --> 17:35.072
Hey, they learned it!

17:35.114 --> 17:36.577
What a bunch of pros.

17:38.451 --> 17:39.746
Hello there, sir.

17:39.788 --> 17:42.083
We are three noblewomen
in distress...

17:42.125 --> 17:43.522
Sorry, boat's full.

17:43.556 --> 17:46.690
Um, what if one of us were
to turn this big ship

17:46.724 --> 17:48.193
into a "tug" boat?

17:48.227 --> 17:49.362
Wink, wink.

17:49.396 --> 17:50.530
Wouldn't help you.

17:50.564 --> 17:52.026
You're the fourth person
to offer that,

17:52.060 --> 17:54.066
and I'm kind of spent.

17:55.700 --> 17:56.833
I don't believe this.

17:56.867 --> 17:59.103
Dear God,
we're stuck here.

17:59.137 --> 18:00.831
<i>We're going to drown
on the Titanic.</i>

18:00.873 --> 18:02.869
Oh, no, we're not.

18:04.732 --> 18:06.999
Hey! I said
you can't come on here.

18:07.033 --> 18:09.196
Get out of my way,
you son of a bitch!

18:14.437 --> 18:15.743
Oh, no!

18:15.777 --> 18:17.944
I can't swim without
my striped one-piece!

18:19.418 --> 18:21.384
Everyone move over.

18:21.418 --> 18:22.712
We're getting on this boat.

18:27.386 --> 18:29.520
Please take my baby!

18:29.562 --> 18:31.857
Yeah, I'm sorry,
if I take your baby,

18:31.891 --> 18:33.729
I'm gonna have
to take everyone's baby.

18:34.995 --> 18:36.465
Can you believe
that woman?

18:36.499 --> 18:39.292
Putting me on the spot
in front of everyone.

18:49.035 --> 18:51.671
To home!

18:51.705 --> 18:53.238
You see what
you started?

18:55.239 --> 18:56.541
Wow, Chris.

18:56.575 --> 18:58.205
You just saved
our lives.

18:58.239 --> 18:59.573
Thank you.

18:59.607 --> 19:01.413
Oh, it was
nothing, Brian.

19:01.447 --> 19:02.748
Listen, Chris,
I, um...

19:02.782 --> 19:04.316
I think I owe
you an apology.

19:04.350 --> 19:07.316
I'm not someone
who often makes mistakes,

19:07.350 --> 19:10.483
but, well, I was wrong earlier
when I called you a moron.

19:10.518 --> 19:12.156
No, you were right.

19:12.190 --> 19:14.727
You're the smart one in the
family, and I'm the dumb one.

19:14.761 --> 19:16.391
Oh, you're being too
hard on yourself.

19:16.433 --> 19:19.894
Look, you may not be
"book smart" or "history smart,"

19:19.936 --> 19:22.564
but you're "throw people
off the boat" smart.

19:22.598 --> 19:24.332
And, frankly,
I'm starting to think

19:24.366 --> 19:26.171
that's the best kind to be.

19:26.205 --> 19:28.034
Thanks, Stewie.

19:34.606 --> 19:37.199
Well, looks like
one ship was saved today--

19:37.233 --> 19:39.229
a relation-ship.

20:00.034 --> 20:01.831
Oh, thank God,
we're back.

20:01.873 --> 20:03.838
Wow, what an
adventure!

20:03.872 --> 20:05.007
Thanks, Dream Brian.

20:05.041 --> 20:06.375
Thanks, Dream Stewie.

20:08.713 --> 20:10.007
Brian, Stewie!

20:10.041 --> 20:12.344
I just had the most
amazing dream.

20:12.378 --> 20:15.215
<i>I was on a famous boat
called the Gigantic.</i>

20:16.385 --> 20:18.249
Yeah, he's gonna fail
that test tomorrow.

20:18.291 --> 20:19.923
Well, we gave it our
best shot, Stewie.

20:19.957 --> 20:22.124
It was still a nice thing
you did for your brother.

20:22.158 --> 20:23.293
I suppose.

20:23.327 --> 20:25.292
I just hope that Chris
killing one extra man

20:25.334 --> 20:27.698
<i>on the Titanic didn't alter
anything in the present.</i>

20:32.897 --> 20:34.200
There he is.

20:34.234 --> 20:36.105
"Nigel Harpington."

20:36.139 --> 20:37.770
Wait, "Harpington"?

20:37.812 --> 20:39.778
Isn't that the name of
Chris' history teacher?

20:39.812 --> 20:41.106
Yes, I think
you're right.

20:41.148 --> 20:42.442
That must be
his ancestor.

20:42.476 --> 20:45.479
Which means that Chris'
history teacher was never born.

20:45.513 --> 20:47.476
Hey, everybody,
I'm Teacher Doug,

20:47.510 --> 20:49.108
and I don't believe
in tests.

20:49.150 --> 20:51.116
Yay!

20:51.150 --> 20:57.650
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru